Day 35 /100 of #DrGVishwanathanChallenge
Today's challenge was all about Backtracking. I worked on two classic problems that strengthened my understanding of recursive exploration, decision-making, and pruning invalid paths.
 Problem 1: LeetCode 39 – Combination Sum
🔹 Approach
Used Backtracking to generate all possible combinations whose sum equals the target.
At every recursive call:
Added the current candidate to the combination.
Continued from the same index since elements can be reused unlimited times.
Backtracked by removing the last element after returning.
Pruned recursion whenever the current sum exceeded the target.
 Time Complexity: O(2<sup>target/min</sup>) (Exponential in the worst case)
Space Complexity: O(target / min) (Recursion depth excluding output)
💡 Key Takeaways
1.Learned how to reuse the same element during recursion.
2. Understood pruning to eliminate unnecessary recursive calls.
3. Improved understanding of recursive state management using backtracking.

Problem 2: LeetCode 78 – Subsets
🔹 Approach
Used Backtracking to generate every possible subset.
Added the current subset to the answer before exploring further.
Iterated from the current index:
Included an element.
Recurred for the remaining elements.
Backtracked by removing the last added element.
Time Complexity: O(N × 2ᴺ)
Space Complexity: O(N) (Recursion stack excluding output)
💡 Key Takeaways
1. Every recursive state represents one valid subset.
2. Backtracking naturally generates the entire power set.
3. Practiced efficient add-remove operations to maintain the current subset.
